    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I fixed `mock_openai()` in `tests/fakes.py`, and both digest tests pass again. One thing needs you: I couldn't delete my scratch test file, `tests/test_zz_tmp.py`, because removing it needs your approval. Please delete it. Until it's gone, `pytest` shows it as one failure. That failure is expected (explained below).\n\n**Why the mocks stopped working:** openai 3.19.2 no longer sends requests through `httpx`. It uses a separate package, `httpx2`. respx 0.23.1 only patches `httpx` and `httpcore`, so the SDK's requests went straight past the mock to the network. Nothing warned because the route just went unused, and the helper sets `assert_all_called=False`.\n\n**The fix** (`tests/fakes.py` only; the helper's interface and `app/` are unchanged):\n- Inside the existing `respx.mock` block, `httpx2.HTTPTransport.handle_request` is patched at the class level with `unittest.mock.patch.object`. The patched method converts each request to an `httpx.Request`, passes it to the same respx router, and converts the reply back to an `httpx2.Response`.\n- Because the patch is on the class, it covers every client: `app.digest`'s cached client, clients created before the block, and clients created inside it. The patch is removed when the block exits.\n- The canned replies, the recorded request bodies, the 400 when replies run out, and respx's error for unmatched URLs all behave as before. Clients given a legacy `httpx.Client` are still caught by respx's own mocks.\n\n**Checked in the scratch test:**\n- A client created before the block, the cached client and a new client all got canned replies.\n- A request after the replies ran out got a 400.\n- A request to an unmocked endpoint raised respx's \"not mocked\" error.\n- After the block, a request went back to the real network. pytest-socket blocked it, and the scratch test lists that as a failure because I expected a different exception type."
  },
